<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cristóbal Mendoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1772">1772</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cristóbal Mendoza was a Venezuelan lawyer and the first holder of executive power in independent Venezuela, as a member of the triumvirate created in March 1811. After the fall of the First Republic he worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, who named him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> in 1813. He later began a large collection of documents on Bolívar's public life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cristobal-mendoza.jpg" alt="Cristóbal Mendoza" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Lovera · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Lovera_2012_007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Cristóbal Hurtado de Mendoza y Montilla was born in Trujillo on 23 June 1772 and was first taught by his father in a Franciscan monastery. At 16 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, where he took a degree in philosophy in 1791, and in 1794 he became a doctor of canon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. After working in law offices in Trujillo and Mérida, he moved to Barinas in late 1796, where he became known for defending local tribal groups, and in January 1807 the Council of Barinas elected him mayor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mendoza joined the movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> elite against the Spanish crown on 19 April 1810 and was elected to represent Barinas in the Constituent Congress. Before he could take his seat he learned he had been named to the triumvirate, whose three members rotated the presidency every week; the other two chose him to go first, on 5 March 1811. He was the author of the Venezuelan Declaration of Independence, issued on 5 July 1811, and took part in drafting the first constitution in December 1811.</p><p>After the First Republic fell to the royalist invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domingo-de-monteverde">Domingo de Monteverde</a>, he became an aide to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, who made him governor of Mérida, which he entered on 23 May 1813. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> he proposed the open meeting of 14 October 1813 at which Bolívar was given the title Liberator. When the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a> took Caracas in July 1814 he went into exile, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> he wrote for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/correo-del-orinoco">Correo del Orinoco</a> under a pseudonym, arguing against monarchies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p>From late 1821 he served as Justice Minister of Gran Colombia. In April 1826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a> made him intendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Venezuela, but he failed to calm the conflict around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a>'s separatist movement, El Cosiata, and Páez exiled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-u-s-virgin-islands">Saint Thomas</a>. Bolívar restored him to the post in 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In poor health, Mendoza resigned in the middle of 1828 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> on 8 February 1829. With Francisco Javier Yanes he had published the first major collection of documents on Bolívar's public life. Since 1972 Venezuela has kept National Lawyer Day on his birthday, 23 June.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cristóbal Mendoza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A federal constitution was adopted in December 1811, with an executive triumvirate including <b>Cristóbal Mendoza</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-venezuela">First Republic of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trujillo, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.37,-70.44&amp;year=1809" aria-label="Trujillo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cristóbal Mendoza"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4124 4203h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4124 4203h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.37,-70.44&amp;year=1809">What was here in 1809? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
